Z||000012||Seanh||12-01-2000||07:09 PM||||Yes, he is planning on have "power" steering. I am also looking into doing this, but not sure if I am going to yet. He is useing a 4.9L Caddy engine, it already has the power steering pump. Running steel lines to the front wouldnt be that hard. <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/transsig.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://www.kdsi.net/~sean" TARGET=_blank>Sean's Fiero Page</A><BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8 Auto<BR>Going on to 3.4 in Jan.<BR><I>"It's always in the last place you look." Of course it is. Why would you keep looking after you've found it? Do people do this? Who and where are they?</I>||||reg||1
Z||000013||rockcrawl||12-01-2000||07:23 PM||||I got the rack on Ebay for something like $26. It's from an '84 Corvette. The Fiero 3 turn rack has 4.5" of travel. The 2.25 turn vette rack has 4" of travel. So if it were used on a Fiero it would be like a 2.53 turn rack compared to a fiero 3 turn rack. The fiero only requires 3.5" to get from full right to full left. They are both "front steer".<P>Here's a picture of both side by side. The black one is from an 84 Vette. Notice the vette rack has a slightly different pinion angle which will put the U-joint very close to the coolant tube.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/both.jpg"> <BR>and here is how they fit the fiero crossmember<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fierorack.jpg"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/vetterack.jpg"> <BR>The red circle below is where the housing would need to be modified. That mount would need to be removed, and a fiero bushing fitted to the housing. <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/both2.jpg"> <P>I removed the bellows from both racks and measured the distance between the inner tie rod pivots. The Vette rack is actually 3.5" shorter, which could cause a bump steer problem. It also may be difficult to find correct length tie rods. The vette tie rods cannot be shortened and re-threaded because they are not thick enough. I'll be scoping out a Merkur rack at the U-Pull-It tomorrow. I believe that's what was used on the Zimmer Quicksilvers and also on Rodney's P/S conversion. I had wanted to keep it GM, but everyone else seems to have used the Merkur rack. Also, my newly aquired Vette rack looks like it was just brought up from the bottom of a lake. At least it's good for the $90 core charge on a rebuilt!<P>I'll be using the P/S pump that is already on the 4.9 liter engine. I will run 3/8" steel lines from front to rear and make connections at the ends using rubber hose. Running these lines is really not difficult, there are already 2 heater pipes, 2 A/C pipes, brake and clutch lines, and 2 large coolant pipes. It would be quite simple to add two more small tubes. The steel tubing can be bought at auto parts in 60" lengths, or in 20 foot lengths at any hydraulic shop.<P>The electric P/S pump is not a bad idea, in fact that's what GM had in mind for the '89 Fiero, BUT, you will not save any weight by going with an electric pump. You will also have the (quite large) added current draw on the electrical system and the need to replace contact tips and motor brushes. I would rather fit a Chevette pump to the Fiero engine and run the lines. <P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||6
Z||000014||mrfixit58||12-04-2000||01:46 PM||||Rock,<P>Thanks for the pics. The hub for the Corvette rack looks like it hangs too low. It appears that it wouldn't clear the front crossmember. Maybe it just an optical illusion but, if it does clear the rack looks like a close fit. <P>Also, I didn't quite understand you statment: "The vette tie rods cannot be shortened and re-threaded because they are not thick enough." I must be missing something... can you explain?<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000015||rockcrawl||12-04-2000||04:55 PM||||Roy,<BR> By hub you mean that round rubber mount that hangs off the bottom of the rack housing?? The part I have circled in red? Yes, it would need to be removed to clear the crossmember. Otherwise, the rack is a very close fit. I am worried about having too much lateral movement of the rack. If I mount it with the fiero bushings and straps as I had planned, there wont be anything to prevent it from sliding in the bushings. The housing doesn't have the recesses for the bushings like the Fero rack does.<P> My other option is to notch and reinforce the crossmember to accept the Vette mounting configuration. It would provide a much firmer mounting. The only possible movement would be the "give" in the rubber bushings.<P>The threads on the tie rods are rolled threads, not cut threads. The part of the rod that is threaded is acually larger in diameter than the rest of the rod because of the rolled threads. If I had a way to thread the rods like they do at the factory, it wouldn't be a problem, but all I have is a die which cuts the rod to make threads resulting in the same outer diameter. If I can get ahold of a chassis parts catalog, I may be able to find tie rods from another GM application that will fit. The other option is cut/sleeve/weld, but that is a last resort.<P>I'm trying to track down a reasonably priced Merkur XR4Ti rack assy. They are hard to find used, but it must be a better fit than the Vette rack, or else why would anyone use it.<P>I'll be out of town all week, so I won't have any chance to work on it.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||7
Z||000016||mrfixit58||12-05-2000||10:16 AM||||Rock,<P>By "hub" I meant the portion of the rack where the steering shaft protrudes. It appears to hang down farther than the factory Fiero rack.<P>I now understand what you ment about the tie rod ends. I wouldn't hesitate using the cut-and-sleeve method. the tie rod shafts only have to steady and turn the wheels and don't require hardened steel. If you are concerned about weakening the metal, you could always have the shafts re-heat treated.<P>As far as securing the rack, have you considered a two piece metal coller and set screw? Personally, it looks like notching the frame would be the easiest thing to do.<P>Keep us posted,<BR>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
